.TH LIMBOREPL 1
.SH NAME
limborepl \- write Limbo programs at a prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B limborepl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Limborepl
reads Limbo source one statement, declaration, or expression at a time
and runs the program it builds up, printing an expression's own value
automatically - roughly the console TempleOS/HolyC gives, though the
mechanism is Limbo's own, not a borrowed one (see PRINTING below).
.PP
It is
.IR disrepl (1)'s
own architecture moved up one compiler stage, from raw Dis assembly to
Limbo source, following
.B doc/limbo-repl-plan.md
in this tree. Nothing here is a new compiler or interpreter:
.I emu
already runs Dis,
.IR limbo (1)
.RB ( /dis/limbo.dis ,
an ordinary Inferno command like any other, not a special case) already
compiles Limbo source into it. What this adds is the loop, and
somewhere for a program to keep its state from one line to the next -
the identical trade
.IR disrepl (1)
already makes: every line typed is kept, and the
.B whole
accumulated program is recompiled and rerun after each new one. A line
with an effect outside the virtual machine happens again on every
subsequent line, for the same reason it does in
.IR disrepl (1).
.SS Declarations, statements, and expressions
Dis assembly has no declarations, so
.IR disrepl (1)
never had to decide where a typed line belongs. Limbo source does:
a line is either a module-scope
.B declaration
(an
.BR adt ,
a bare typed variable, an
.B include
directive, or a complete one-line function definition), an ordinary
.B statement
that runs inside
.BR init() 's
own body, or a bare
.B expression
(see PRINTING). Each typed line is classified by a deliberately rough
heuristic, not parsed properly - see BUGS for exactly what it does and
does not recognise.
.PP
A function has to be written as ONE line, braces, semicolons and all:
.IP
.EX
square(n: int): int { return n*n; }
.EE
.PP
An extra module works the same way any Limbo program uses one - an
.B include
line and a variable declaration (both go to module scope), then a
.B load
call (an ordinary statement):
.IP
.EX
include "string.m";
str: String;
str = load String String->PATH;
sys->print("%d\en", str->toint("42", 10).t0);
.EE
.PP
.B Sys
is already loaded before any typed statement runs, so
.B sys->print(...)
works immediately with no
.B load
of your own.
.SS Redeclaration
Limbo forbids declaring the same name with
.B :=
twice in one scope, and the whole session is one scope once assembled.
This program does not detect or rewrite that for you - write
.B x = 6;
the second time, not another
.BR "x := 6;" .
Typing the same
.B :=
twice fails to compile like any other Limbo redeclaration error, and
the line is rejected exactly like any other line that does not compile
- see below.
.SS Printing
A line ending in
.B ;
or
.B }
runs as an ordinary statement, exactly as before. A line that does
.B not
end that way is a bare expression, and its value is printed
automatically:
.IP
.EX
limbo> x := 6;
limbo> y := 7;
limbo> x*y
42
.EE
.PP
This claims syntax that used to be simply rejected, not something that
used to work a different way: a line with neither a trailing
.B ;
(required of an ordinary statement) nor a trailing
.B }
(the one way a control construct or bare block gets away without one)
was already a compile error before this. What runs behind it is one
line, rewritten in place - no temporary variable, no block:
.IP
.EX
sys->print("%s\en", string (EXPR));
.EE
.PP
using Limbo's own built-in cast of an arithmetic value
.RB ( byte ", " int ", " big ", or " real )
or a
.B string
itself to
.BR string ,
which is legal directly as a call argument - confirmed against the
real compiler - so there is nothing for a temporary to buy. (An
earlier version of this file introduced one anyway, plus a
.B { }
to scope it; this is source-level ahead-of-time compilation, the same
as the rest of the session, and does not need it.) A value of any
other type -
.BR list ", " array ", " adt ,
a channel, a function value - fails to compile with Limbo's own
.B "cannot make a string from ..."
diagnostic, rejected and not kept exactly like any other line that does
not compile; call
.B print()
yourself and end the line with
.B ;
for those, exactly as before this was added.
.PP
This inherits the one consequence of replaying the whole session on
every new line (see DESCRIPTION above): an auto-printed value is not
printed once and forgotten, it is printed again every time a later
line triggers a fresh rebuild-and-rerun, since the print line is still
there in the accumulated program. A five-line session with
three auto-printed expressions in it therefore prints far more than
three lines by the end - each one reappearing, in order, on every
later run. This is the same trade the rest of the session already
makes, not something particular to auto-printing, but it is far more
visible here than it was when nothing printed itself by default.
.SS Commands
.TP
.B .list
Show the program so far, in the order it was typed.
.TP
.B .drop
Remove the last line (declaration or statement, whichever was typed
most recently) and run again.
.TP
.B .clear
Start again.
.TP
.B .src
Show the Limbo source that would be compiled.
.TP
.B !
Stop a program that is still running.
.TP
.B .quit
Leave.
.SS Stopping a program
Typing
.B !
while a program is running stops it, for the same reason
.IR disrepl (1)
needs it: on this platform
.I emu
wires the host interrupt signal to a clean exit, so the usual interrupt
character takes down the whole emulator, session and all, rather than
just the one running program. The interrupt is a line of input like any
other, arriving on the same channel and selected against the running
program in one
.BR alt .
Input that arrives while a program is running and is not
.B !
is queued rather than acted on, so a session can be piped in without
its next line being mistaken for an interrupt.
.PP
A program that runs for more than three seconds is stopped anyway, for
when nobody is watching - the same backstop
.IR disrepl (1)
has, for the same reason: a typed infinite loop is not hypothetical,
and confirmed directly during this program's own development (see
BUGS).
.PP
A line that does not compile, or that has to be stopped, is not kept -
a mistake would otherwise fail every line typed after it. A line that
compiles and runs but raises an exception is kept, because it did
something real.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/limborepl.b
the Limbo source of the current session
.TP
.B /tmp/limborepl.dis
the module compiled from it
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/limborepl.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR disrepl (1),
.IR limbo (1),
.IR prog (3)
.PP
.B doc/limbo-repl-plan.md
in this tree, written before this file and followed directly - the
design reasoning, including the two options considered for
redeclaration and why the simpler one (require
.B =
yourself) was chosen for this first version, lives there rather than
being repeated here.
.SH BUGS
The declaration/statement classifier is deliberately rough, not a real
parse. What it recognises as a declaration: a line beginning
.BR "include \(dq...\(dq;" ;
a line of the shape
.IB name : rest
- or
.IB name ", " name "(, " name ")*: " rest ,
Limbo's own multi-name shape such as
.B "x, y: int;"
-
where
.I rest
does not itself begin with
.BR for ,
.BR while ,
.BR case ,
.BR alt ,
.BR pick ,
or
.B do
(that exclusion matters - a labeled loop like
.B "top: for(;;){...}"
is a
.B statement,
not a declaration, and the two are told apart only by what follows the
colon); and a line beginning
.IB name (
whose own brace count balances back to zero by the end of the line (a
one-line function definition, told apart from an ordinary function-call
.I statement
like
.B "foo(1, 2, 3);"
only by the presence of a brace at all). Anything else is a statement.
The multi-name case was a real gap found by review, not by any test
failing: without the comma scan,
.B "x, y: int;"
stopped at the comma, fell through as an ordinary statement, and
silently compiled anyway, since the same text is
.I also
legal Limbo inside a function body - just a local instead of the
module-scope global the line asked for, wrong in a way nothing pointed
at until someone read the classifier expecting it to handle this shape
and it did not. Confirmed by a negative control: reverting the comma
scan reproduces exactly this misfiling.  This still misclassifies at
least one real, if unusual, shape: a module-scope declaration that
itself happens to end in
.BR for / while /etc
textually (there is none in ordinary use, but the heuristic does not
know that) would be misfiled as a statement. Multi-line declarations of
any kind - a function body spanning more than one line, an
.B adt
written across several - are not supported at all in this version; they
have to be written on one line, which is workable for small examples
and unpleasant for anything larger.
.PP
The auto-print convention (see PRINTING) reads a trailing
.B ;
or
.B }
as "this is a statement" purely by looking at the last character of the
typed line, so a line that was meant as an ordinary
.B :=
declaration but has its trailing
.B ;
left off by mistake is not caught as a typo - it compiles anyway, as an
expression, because Limbo itself allows
.B :=
to appear as an expression, not only as a statement. Typing
.B "x := 5"
with the semicolon left off prints
.B 5
as if it had worked, and - because the value is printed by a rewritten
line in place rather than inside a temporary's own block (see
PRINTING) -
.B x
.I does
persist to the next line, exactly as if the semicolon had been there:
confirmed directly, not assumed, by typing
.B "x := 5"
with no semicolon and then
.B "x = 6;"
and
.B x
on later lines, which reassigns and then prints
.B 6
rather than failing to find
.BR x .
Typing the same
.B :=
line twice, though, still fails exactly as any other redeclaration
does (see REDECLARATION above) - the missing semicolon does not create
a second, independently scoped
.BR x .
.PP
A line that compiles and runs but raises an exception is kept in the
session rather than discarded, as stated above - confirmed directly,
not just asserted: typing an out-of-bounds array read leaves the
session raising the same exception on every later line, exactly as
keeping it implies, and
.B .drop
recovers cleanly once enough lines are dropped to remove the raising
line itself, with no special-casing needed to make that work.
.PP
The three-second timeout was tested directly during development, not
assumed from
.IR disrepl (1)'s
own precedent: typing
.B "for(;;);"
was confirmed to be stopped at three seconds, the line correctly
rejected (not kept), and the session continuing normally on the next
line - the same verification
.IR disrepl (1)'s
own man page describes for its own timeout, repeated here rather than
inherited on faith since compiling real Limbo source is a genuinely
different code path from assembling Dis instructions.
.PP
Re-compiling the whole session after each line is quadratic in the
length of the session, and worse in practice than
.IR disrepl (1)'s
own equivalent cost - a real
.I limbo
compile is heavier than assembling already-accumulated instructions.
Fine for the short interactive sessions this is for; not addressed
here.
.PP
There is no history, no editing beyond what the terminal provides, and
no way to load a session from a file or save one to it - the same
limitations
.IR disrepl (1)
has, unaddressed for the same reason: out of scope for a first version
built to test whether the architecture carries over at all, which it
does.
